9.2 Gaussian (Bures) Wasserstein — the right metric on DeCovarT objects
For two non-degenerate Gaussians \(P_{0}=\mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{m}_{0},\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{0})\) and \(P_{1}=\mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{m}_{1},\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{1})\), the squared 2-Wasserstein distance with quadratic cost splits as
\[
W_{2}^{2}(P_{0},P_{1})
=
\|\boldsymbol{m}_{0}-\boldsymbol{m}_{1}\|_{2}^{2}
+
\operatorname{tr}\Bigl(
\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{0}+\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{1}
-2
\bigl(
\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{0}^{1/2}
\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{1}
\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{0}^{1/2}
\bigr)^{1/2}
\Bigr).
\tag{9.1}\]
The second term is the Bures–Wasserstein metric on SPD covariances. The Monge map is affine (Brenier):
\[
T(\boldsymbol{x})
=
\boldsymbol{m}_{1}
+
A(\boldsymbol{x}-\boldsymbol{m}_{0}),
\qquad
A
=
\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{1}^{1/2}
\bigl(
\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{1}^{1/2}
\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{0}
\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{1}^{1/2}
\bigr)^{-1/2}
\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{1}^{1/2},
\tag{9.2}\]
when the covariances are invertible. No discrete cell-to-cell OT is required merely to transport one fitted type-level Gaussian into another, or to transport a Gaussian approximation of the cell cloud onto the exported \(\mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\cdot j},\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{j})\).
Entropic OT between Gaussians remains closed form (Janati et al. 2020) and stays well behaved for degenerate covariances — relevant when \(G&gt;n_{j}\) before a GGM projection.
Core use. \(W_{2}\) between (i) the empirical Gaussian of cells of type \(j\) (or a shrinkage covariance) and (ii) the exported GGM / PLN Gaussian is the alignment score of Chapter 8. Large Bures discrepancy with small mean discrepancy means the graph changed the covariance geometry; the opposite means we only moved the centroid.

9.3 Wasserstein-robust inverse covariance
Nguyen, Kuhn and Mohajerin Esfahani (Nguyen et al. 2018) estimate a precision by minimising a worst-case Stein loss over normal laws within a Wasserstein ball of the empirical normal \(\mathcal{N}(\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}},\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}})\). Without structural constraints the estimator is an analytical nonlinear shrinkage of the sample eigenvalues: invertible for \(G&gt;n\), rotation-equivariant, order-preserving. With conditional-independence constraints it becomes a sequential quadratic programme for a GGM.
That is more directly relevant to DeCovarT than OT-as-alignment theatre: it regularises \(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{j}\) on the SPD manifold that the likelihood uses. A composition (improved pipeline; proposal) is
\[
\text{technical type-level covariances}
\;\longrightarrow\;
\text{Bures / Wasserstein shrinkage or barycentre}
\;\longrightarrow\;
\text{sparse precision projection}.
\tag{9.3}\]
The barycentre of several bootstrap covariances in Bures geometry is a principled “average graph-aware covariance” when we do not want to average precisions (which does not commute with inversion either).
Adversarial alignment notes and scConfluence are not default aligners: they target multi-omics / embedding matching, which we excluded from the core. CARDAMOM-style OT is useful only insofar as the cost remains quadratic in the same gene space.

10.1 Technical bootstrap (available)
Resample cells with replacement within type, labels fixed. Each replicate yields \((\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}^{\ast},\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Theta}}^{\ast})\) and therefore:

edge-inclusion frequencies (Chapter 8);
a cloud of SPD covariances whose Bures barycentre is a candidate export;
Monte Carlo error for MixSim overlap and for DeCovarT \(\hat{\boldsymbol{p}}\) in downstream simulations.

This is uncertainty conditional on this gastruloid experiment. It does not stand in for organoid-to-organoid variability (Squair et al. 2021). Calling technical pseudo-bulks “biological replicates” is the error already flagged in Note 5.2.

8.2 Likelihood and information criteria
On the Gaussian scale actually used by the estimator,

eBIC (extended BIC) is the usual glasso selection criterion when FDR methods are not used;
held-out log-likelihood on cells left out within type (still technical, not biological);
SILGGM’s own global FDR and, when a simulator supplies a true skeleton, power and Matthews correlation (the package’s simulation diagnostics).

These scores compare nested Gaussian models. They do not validate a PLN latent graph against a glasso graph on NPN data: the observation models differ. Cross-family comparison must move to a common object — the exported \((\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\cdot j},\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{j})\) on the linear DeCovarT scale — and use distributional distances.

9.2 Graph summaries that do not need a reference
These describe shape, not correctness:

degree distribution and a power-law diagnostic (SILGGM used \(\log\)–\(\log\) degree plots as a biological sanity check; many biological networks are not strictly scale-free);
hub overlap across types (rewiring of hubs is a DeCovarT-relevant summary);
clustering coefficient, number of connected components, modularity;
overlap coefficients of edge sets between methods, as in GRETA (intersection over the smaller set), which avoids punishing a method for returning a larger graph.

GRETA’s most uncomfortable topological result should be treated as a prior: edge-level overlap across methods is tiny even when TF and gene sets overlap moderately. If our four core estimators disagree on edges, that is the expected state of GRN inference, not a bug unique to gastruloids. Downstream DeCovarT should therefore not depend on a single edge being true; it should depend on the SPD covariance being a stable discriminator.
